WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.830
In this video, we'll look at utilizing

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.230
the packaged build of the project

3
00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.189
to understand better how OSC functions.

4
00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:11.429
When you look at the extracted folder

5
00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:12.650
that came with this project,

6
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.070
you'll look in the packaged folder and find that

7
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.710
there's an executable file as well as two batch files.

8
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.750
Now to start, if you just want to test

9
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.449
sending what we call one-way communication,

10
00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:25.469
where the sender is sending

11
00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:28.109
data and being received by another computer,

12
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:31.589
all you need to do is open up the sender batch file

13
00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:34.090
and change the receiver IP address

14
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.350
to the local IPv4 address

15
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.630
of the computer you're on right now.

16
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:46.310
Using the IP config, as June demonstrated earlier,

17
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:50.009
you'll then find that with two instances open

18
00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:52.070
on the same computer or two computers

19
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.770
on the same local network,

20
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.509
you'll find that whoever has the sender batch file opened

21
00:00:57.509 --> 00:01:00.609
will be able to affect the scene,

22
00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:02.810
changing both the scale of the cubes,

23
00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.260
whether they're on and off or the colors of them.

24
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.299
If you would like to use the packaged builds

25
00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:11.379
and test two-way functionality,

26
00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:13.180
if you're on the same subnet

27
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.980
on the same local area network,

28
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.700
you'll just need to make sure that both the sender IP

29
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.900
and receiver IP is set up.

30
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.459
And again, on a local subnet,

31
00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:24.099
you can just use the IPv4 addresses

32
00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:27.700
that show up when you type in IP config on each computer.

33
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.219
However, if you want to make sure

34
00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:31.159
that this will work over the internet,

35
00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:33.879
then you will actually need to utilize using something

36
00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:36.599
like what's my IP address in Google

37
00:01:36.599 --> 00:01:41.439
to find out what the WAN is for your actual IP.

38
00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:44.000
And then that data would be put into here

39
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.439
alongside making sure that your port forwarding

40
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.400
has already been completed to open up communication

41
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.260
between those two computers across different networks.

42
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.159
Please also ensure that the final launch parameter

43
00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:59.260
dash osc2a is set to equal true.

44
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.040
Otherwise it will still be set up

45
00:02:01.519 --> 00:02:05.769
to only send data in one direction.

46
00:02:05.769 --> 00:02:06.930
So now on the screen,

47
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.610
we're seeing that the right-hand side

48
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.210
is my local computer in my apartment.

49
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.409
And on the left side,

50
00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:17.289
we're running this app on a office computer.

51
00:02:17.289 --> 00:02:19.650
So now we can see that when the office computer

52
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.449
is controlling the pink bar,

53
00:02:21.449 --> 00:02:24.250
we see a lot of the print strings.

54
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.490
It's just saying that I received a message.

55
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.810
The address is float slash Z and the value.

56
00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:35.849
And if I do it, it's also printing on the other side.

57
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.849
So that's how we can make sure.

58
00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:41.870
Well, and also the pink bar moves with the location.

59
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.810
So that's how we can confirm we're receiving the messages.

60
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.210
And now if I change the color of the cubes,

61
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.659
it will also change on the other side.

62
00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:55.580
And as you can see,

63
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.120
all these are not happening inside a multiplayer session.

64
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.379
So we are just like,

65
00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:02.180
we are only controlling the cubes,

66
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.819
but not replicating actors or,

67
00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.030
or sorry, but not replicating the character movements.

68
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.689
So we can be very selective about what are the things

69
00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:17.030
we want to communicate and what are not.

70
00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:22.319
We have the Boolean messages here,

71
00:03:25.419 --> 00:03:27.319
integer messages here.

72
00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:29.419
Oh, it's off.

73
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.509
And a float message from here.

74
00:03:34.509 --> 00:03:36.870
And because this is set up for two way communication

75
00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.349
right now, I also have the same capabilities

76
00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:40.389
as June does.

77
00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:43.469
So I am also able to do things like change color

78
00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:45.629
or turn the cubes off and on.
